/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ai-200.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ai-200", [["az1",0,1,"A team builds a Python API image on every commit. Developers do not have Docker installed, and the build must run in Azure. Which command should the pipeline run?",["az acr build --registry myreg --image api:{{.Run.ID}} .","docker build -t myreg.azurecr.io/api .","az acr import --name myreg --source api:latest","az containerapp up --source . --ingress internal"],0,"az acr build is an ACR quick task: it uploads the context and builds and pushes the image in Azure without a local Docker engine. docker build needs local Docker, and az acr import copies an existing image rather than building one.","Skills 1: ACR"],["az2",0,1,"A company wants all its container images rebuilt automatically when Microsoft publishes a patched Python base image. What should the developer configure?",["A Container Apps scale rule on image age","An ACR task with a base image update trigger","A retention policy for untagged manifests","Geo-replication of the registry to a second Azure region"],1,"ACR Tasks can track the base image in a Dockerfile and rebuild the app image when that base image is updated. Retention policies and geo-replication do not rebuild anything.","Skills 1: ACR Tasks"],["az3",0,1,"A registry must be reachable only through a private endpoint in a virtual network and replicated to a second region. Which ACR tier is required?",["Basic","Standard","Premium","Any tier with admin user enabled"],2,"Private endpoints and geo-replication are Premium features. Basic and Standard differ in storage and throughput but do not offer these.","Skills 1: ACR"],["az4",0,1,"A Container App must pull images from ACR without any stored password. What is the recommended setup?",["Enable the ACR admin user and store its password as a Container Apps secret","Make the registry repository public for the app's IP range","Create a service principal and paste its secret into an environment variable","Give the app's managed identity the AcrPull role on the registry"],3,"A managed identity with AcrPull removes stored secrets and grants only pull rights. The admin user and service principal both leave a secret to store and rotate.","Skills 1: ACR authentication"],["az5",0,1,"Operations wants production to run exactly the image that passed testing, even if someone later pushes a new image with the same tag. How should the image be referenced?",["By the latest tag","By its manifest digest","By the repository name only","By a tag that includes the registry name"],1,"A digest (sha256:...) identifies one immutable manifest. Tags, including latest, can be moved to point at a different image.","Skills 1: Tags and digests"],["az6",0,1,"A custom Linux container on App Service listens on port 8000, and the site returns errors because the platform cannot reach the app. Which app setting fixes this?",["WEBSITES_PORT=8000","WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE=1","SCM_DO_BUILD_DURING_DEPLOYMENT=true","FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME=python"],0,"WEBSITES_PORT tells App Service which container port to forward traffic to. The other settings control package deployment, build on deploy and Functions runtime.","Skills 1: App Service containers"],["az7",0,1,"A team wants to send 10% of users to a new version of a Container App while 90% stay on the current one. What must they configure?",["Single revision mode with a new image tag","A KEDA HTTP scale rule with 10 concurrent requests","Multiple revision mode with traffic weights on each revision","Two Container Apps environments behind one shared external ingress endpoint"],2,"Traffic splitting between revisions needs multiple revision mode, where each active revision gets a percentage. Single revision mode moves all traffic to the newest revision.","Skills 1: Container Apps revisions"],["az8",0,1,"A background worker in Container Apps reads a Service Bus queue. It should run zero replicas when the queue is empty and add replicas as the backlog grows. What should be configured?",["An HTTP scale rule with min replicas 1 and max replicas 10 on the worker","A custom azure-servicebus scale rule with min replicas 0","A CPU scale rule with min replicas 0","A fixed replica count of 5"],1,"The KEDA azure-servicebus scaler scales on queue length and can scale to zero when min replicas is 0. CPU rules cannot scale to zero, and HTTP rules only react to incoming requests.","Skills 1: Container Apps scaling"],["az9",0,1,"An internal API in Container Apps must be callable only by other apps in the same environment, not from the internet. How should ingress be set?",["Disable ingress and use Dapr only","External ingress with IP restrictions","Internal ingress","External ingress on port 443 with a custom domain"],2,"Internal ingress exposes the app only inside the Container Apps environment (and its virtual network). External ingress is reachable from the internet even with extra restrictions.","Skills 1: Container Apps ingress"],["az10",0,1,"A nightly batch step should run in a container at 02:00, finish, and stop billing until the next run. Which Container Apps feature fits best?",["A scheduled Container Apps job","A Container App with min replicas 1","An App Service WebJob in a custom container","A second revision with 0% traffic"],0,"Container Apps jobs run to completion; a scheduled job uses a cron expression and does not keep a replica running between runs. A Container App with min replicas 1 bills all day.","Skills 1: Container Apps jobs"],["az11",0,1,"An AKS Deployment's pods are stuck with ErrImagePull when pulling from a private ACR. What is the simplest fix?",["Add a ConfigMap with the registry URL","Increase the pod memory limit","Change the Service type to LoadBalancer","Run az aks update --attach-acr"],3,"--attach-acr gives the cluster's kubelet identity the AcrPull role on the registry. ConfigMaps, memory limits and Service types do not affect image pulls.","Skills 1: AKS"],["az12",0,1,"A Kubernetes Deployment manifest should keep three copies of an API pod running. Which field sets this?",["spec.replicas","spec.template.spec.containers.count","metadata.labels.replicas","spec.strategy.maxSurge"],0,"spec.replicas sets the desired number of pods. maxSurge only controls how many extra pods may be created during a rolling update.","Skills 1: AKS manifests"],["az13",0,1,"A Python image is 1.2 GB because it includes compilers used only to build wheels. How can the developer shrink the final image?",["Add more layers with RUN rm commands at the end","Use a multi-stage build with a slim final image","Change the tag from latest to slim","Enable ACR geo-replication"],1,"A multi-stage build keeps build tools in an earlier stage and copies only the output into a small final image. Deleting files in a later layer does not shrink earlier layers.","Skills 1: Dockerfiles"],["az14",0,1,"Which statement about Container Apps secrets is correct?",["Secrets are stored in the container image and read at build time","Secrets can only be plain text typed into the portal","Secrets are shared automatically with every app in the environment","Secrets belong to one app and can reference Key Vault"],3,"Container Apps secrets belong to one app and can be Key Vault references resolved with a managed identity. They are not baked into images or shared across apps.","Skills 1: Container Apps secrets"],["az15",0,1,"A web app on App Service runs a custom container. The team wants to test a new image in production conditions and swap it live with no downtime. What should they use?",["A deployment slot, then swap","A second App Service plan","Scale out to two instances","A new ACR webhook on the production slot"],0,"Deployment slots let you deploy and warm up a new version, then swap it with production. Scaling out or adding a plan does not give a staged swap.","Skills 1: App Service containers"],["az16",0,1,"Which statement correctly describes Azure Container Apps environments?",["Each app must be in its own environment","Environments are Kubernetes clusters you manage with kubectl","Apps in an environment share networking and logging","Environments exist only for Container Apps jobs"],2,"An environment is a secure boundary around a group of apps that share networking and Log Analytics settings. Microsoft manages the underlying Kubernetes; you do not use kubectl on it.","Skills 1: Container Apps"],["az17",0,1,"An ACR repository holds thousands of old image versions, and storage costs keep rising. What should the developer set up?",["Geo-replication to spread storage across regions","A scheduled acr purge task with an age filter","Content trust on the repository","A new registry for each release"],1,"acr purge in a scheduled task deletes tags and manifests older than a set age that match a filter. Geo-replication adds copies and raises storage.","Skills 1: ACR"],["az18",0,1,"In a Kubernetes Deployment, what is the effect of setting resources.requests for CPU and memory?",["The scheduler places the pod only on a node with that much unreserved capacity","The container is killed as soon as its usage reaches that amount of CPU or memory","The pod gets a public IP address","The image is pulled only once per node"],0,"Requests are what the scheduler reserves when placing a pod. Limits, not requests, cap usage (a container over its memory limit is killed).","Skills 1: AKS manifests"],["az19",0,1,"A developer wants to run a new Container App revision for testing through its own URL without sending it any production traffic. What can they use?",["An internal ingress on a second environment","A KEDA rule with min replicas 0","Single revision mode with a new suffix","A revision label with 0% traffic weight"],3,"In multiple revision mode, a label gives a revision its own stable URL, and a 0% weight keeps it out of the main traffic split. Single revision mode would move all traffic to it.","Skills 1: Container Apps revisions"],["az20",0,1,"Why should a container run as a non-root user?",["It makes the image build faster","It is required for ACR to accept the push","It limits damage if an attacker breaks into the process","It lets the container listen on port 80 without extra settings"],2,"A non-root user reduces what an attacker can do after compromising the app. It is not required by ACR, and binding to ports below 1024 is actually harder without root.","Skills 1: Dockerfiles"],["az21",0,1,"A team deploys the same image to Container Apps in three regions and wants pulls to stay in-region for speed. What should they use?",["Three Basic registries kept in sync with a nightly copy script","A Premium registry with geo-replication to each region","A Standard registry with a CDN in front","An ACR token per region"],1,"Geo-replication on Premium keeps one registry name while serving pulls from a nearby replica. Separate registries need manual syncing, and ACR does not sit behind a CDN.","Skills 1: ACR"],["az22",0,2,"An app reads a user profile by id many times per second, and the container is partitioned by /userId. Which approach costs the fewest RUs?",["A query with SELECT * FROM c WHERE c.id = @id AND c.userId = @uid","A cross-partition query with ORDER BY","A point read using read_item(item=id, partition_key=userId)","A change feed read filtered by id"],2,"A point read with the id and partition key is the cheapest operation, about 1 RU for a 1 KB item. A query for the same item costs more because the engine must process it.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB SDK"],["az23",0,2,"A multi-tenant SaaS app stores documents in Cosmos DB. Most queries filter by tenantId, and some tenants are far larger than others. Which partition key choice avoids hot partitions while keeping queries targeted?",["A hierarchical partition key such as /tenantId then /userId","/country, because it has few values","/createdDate, because it grows over time","/type, because it cleanly separates the different kinds of documents"],0,"A hierarchical key keeps tenant queries targeted while spreading large tenants across more partitions. Low-cardinality or date keys concentrate writes on a few partitions.","Skills 2: Partitioning"],["az24",0,2,"Each item in a Cosmos DB container stores a 1,536-dimension embedding at /embedding. Write RU cost is high. What should the developer change?",["Switch the account to Strong consistency","Exclude /embedding/* from the range index and use a vector index for that path","Remove the partition key","Store the embedding as a string instead"],1,"Indexing every number of an embedding in the normal index wastes RUs; the path should be excluded and covered by a vector index instead. Strong consistency adds cost rather than reducing it.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search"],["az25",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB for NoSQL query returns the five items most similar to a query vector @v?",["SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c ORDER BY VectorDistance(c.embedding, @v)","SELECT c.id FROM c WHERE c.embedding = @v LIMIT 5","SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c WHERE ARRAY_CONTAINS(c.embedding, @v, true)","SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c ORDER BY c.embedding DESC"],0,"VectorDistance computes similarity using the container's vector policy, and ORDER BY with TOP returns the nearest items. Equality, ARRAY_CONTAINS and plain ORDER BY do not compare vectors by similarity.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search"],["az26",0,2,"A developer creates a new container for embeddings. Which setting must be defined in the container vector policy?",["The consistency level","The throughput in RU/s","The TTL for each vector","The embedding path, data type, dimensions and distance function"],3,"The vector policy describes each embedding path's data type, dimensions and distance function. Consistency is set on the account, and throughput and TTL are separate container settings.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search"],["az27",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB vector index type is designed for large collections and gives fast approximate search with low latency at scale?",["flat","range","diskANN","spatial"],2,"diskANN is the approximate nearest neighbor index built for large vector sets. flat does exact search and suits small sets; range and spatial are not vector indexes.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search"],["az28",0,2,"New product descriptions are written to Cosmos DB. An embedding must be generated for each new or updated item without polling. What should the developer use?",["A timer trigger that queries all items for changes once every minute","An Azure Functions Cosmos DB trigger based on the change feed","A Cosmos DB stored procedure called by the client","Strong consistency with a read-after-write"],1,"The Cosmos DB trigger reads the change feed and runs for each batch of new or changed items. A timer that queries everything is polling and costs far more RUs.","Skills 2: Change feed"],["az29",0,2,"A change feed processor runs on three instances. How do the instances avoid processing the same changes twice?",["Each instance reads a different database","They use Strong consistency","Each instance filters by a random number","They share a lease container that tracks which instance owns each partition range"],3,"The lease container stores ownership and checkpoints for each feed range so work is split and progress survives restarts. Consistency level does not coordinate consumers.","Skills 2: Change feed"],["az30",0,2,"A developer notices that deleted items never show up in the change feed in latest version mode. What is a common way to handle deletes?",["Use a soft-delete flag and let TTL remove the item later","Switch to Eventual consistency","Read the change feed from the beginning each time","Use a stored procedure that raises an error whenever an item is deleted"],0,"Latest version mode does not include deletes, so a soft-delete field (an update the feed sees) plus TTL is a standard pattern. All versions and deletes mode is the other option.","Skills 2: Change feed"],["az31",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB consistency level is the default and gives read-your-own-writes within a client session at lower cost than Strong?",["Eventual","Session","Consistent prefix","Bounded staleness"],1,"Session consistency is the default and guarantees a client sees its own writes. Strong and bounded staleness cost more RUs for reads; eventual and consistent prefix give weaker guarantees.","Skills 2: Consistency"],["az32",0,2,"A query in the Python SDK filters on a user-supplied category. How should the value be passed?",["Build the SQL string with an f-string","Put the value in the partition key","Use parameters=[{'name': '@cat', 'value': category}] with WHERE c.category = @cat","Escape quotes manually before concatenating"],2,"Parameterized queries prevent injection and let the service reuse the query plan. String building with f-strings or manual escaping is unsafe.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB SDK"],["az33",0,2,"On Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server, CREATE EXTENSION vector fails with a message that the extension is not allowed. What must the developer do first?",["Upgrade to the Memory Optimized tier","Install the pgvector package with pip on the client machine first","Enable pgBouncer","Add VECTOR to the azure.extensions server parameter"],3,"Flexible server only allows extensions listed in the azure.extensions server parameter. pgvector is a server extension, not a client package, and the tier does not matter.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector"],["az34",0,2,"In pgvector, which operator returns cosine distance between two vectors?",["<=>","<->","<#>","@@"],0,"<=> is cosine distance, <-> is Euclidean (L2) distance and <#> is negative inner product. @@ is PostgreSQL full-text match.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector"],["az35",0,2,"A table of 2 million document chunks needs fast approximate nearest neighbor search with high recall, and rows keep arriving. Which pgvector index is the better fit?",["A B-tree index on the embedding column","HNSW with vector_cosine_ops","IVFFlat created on the empty table before loading","A GIN index on the text column"],1,"HNSW gives high recall and fast queries and can be built on an empty table or as data arrives. IVFFlat should be built after data is loaded so its lists reflect the data; B-tree and GIN do not index vector similarity.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector"],["az36",0,2,"An HNSW index returns fast results, but some relevant chunks are missing. Which setting can raise recall at query time?",["Lower ivfflat.probes","Reduce the vector dimensions","Increase hnsw.ef_search","Drop and recreate the index with m = 2"],2,"hnsw.ef_search sets how many candidates the search keeps, so a higher value improves recall at some speed cost. probes applies to IVFFlat, and a tiny m lowers recall.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector"],["az37",0,2,"A RAG app must only return chunks that belong to the signed-in customer. How should retrieval be written in PostgreSQL?",["Filter results in the prompt by asking the model to ignore other customers","Store each customer's chunks in a separate embedding model","Sort by customer_id before the vector search","Add WHERE customer_id = %s to the vector similarity query"],3,"Metadata filtering in the query guarantees that only allowed rows are retrieved. Asking the model to ignore data is not a security control.","Skills 2: RAG"],["az38",0,2,"What is the correct order of steps when ingesting documents for RAG?",["Chunk the text, create embeddings, store text, embeddings and metadata","Create one embedding for the whole library, then chunk it","Send whole documents to the model, then store the answers","Store the full text first, then embed each user question and save it in the table"],0,"Documents are split into chunks, each chunk is embedded, and text, vectors and metadata are stored for retrieval. Questions are embedded at query time, not stored as the corpus.","Skills 2: RAG"],["az39",0,2,"A product catalog API reads from PostgreSQL on every request, and read latency is too high. Prices change a few times a day. What should the developer implement with Azure Managed Redis?",["Write-behind caching of all database writes","Cache-aside with a TTL and deletion of the key when a price changes","Store the catalog only in Redis with no TTL","Use Redis as the primary database for the catalog with persistence turned off"],1,"Cache-aside reads the cache first, fills it on a miss, and uses a TTL plus invalidation on change to keep data fresh. Making Redis the only store risks data loss.","Skills 2: Redis caching"],["az40",0,2,"A chatbot sends many questions that are worded differently but mean the same thing, and model costs are high. Which Redis-based technique reduces repeated model calls?",["Key expiration on session tokens","Pub/Sub channels for each user","A semantic cache that stores responses keyed by the question's embedding","A sorted set of question lengths"],2,"A semantic cache looks up earlier answers whose question embeddings are close to the new one, so similar questions reuse a response. The other features do not match by meaning.","Skills 2: Redis vector search"],["az41",0,2,"Redis memory is full, and the cache should drop the least recently used keys that have a TTL while keeping keys without one. Which eviction policy fits?",["volatile-lru","allkeys-random","noeviction","allkeys-lfu"],0,"volatile-lru evicts least recently used keys among those with an expiry. allkeys policies can evict any key, and noeviction returns errors on writes when memory is full.","Skills 2: Redis caching"],["az42",0,2,"A team's documents are already in relational tables with foreign keys and they need RAG with joins and filters on those tables. Which store is the best fit for the vectors?",["Azure Managed Redis as the only store","A new Cosmos DB account with the documents copied","Azure Blob Storage with index tags","Azure Database for PostgreSQL with pgvector"],3,"pgvector keeps vectors next to the relational data so SQL joins and filters work in one query. Copying to another store adds sync work, and Redis is best as a cache in front of a source of truth.","Skills 2: Choosing a store"],["az43",0,2,"A Python app gets HTTP 429 responses from Cosmos DB during bursts. What does this mean, and what is a good first response?",["The partition key is missing; recreate the container","Provisioned RU/s were exceeded; let the SDK retry after the given delay and review RU use or autoscale","The consistency level is too weak; switch to Strong","The item is larger than 2 MB; split the item"],1,"429 means the request rate is too large for the provisioned throughput. The SDK retries using the retry-after header; persistent 429s call for query tuning or more (autoscale) RU/s.","Skills 2: Request Units"],["az44",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB indexing policy change lowers write RU cost for items with many properties that are never queried?",["Switch to Strong consistency","Add a composite index on every property","Keep the default policy that automatically indexes every path","Include only the queried paths and exclude the rest"],3,"Indexing fewer paths means less index maintenance on every write. The default indexes everything, and composite indexes add cost.","Skills 2: Request Units"],["az45",0,2,"When is Azure Managed Redis a poor choice as the place to store embeddings?",["When vectors need millisecond lookups","When the vectors are only a cache in front of another durable store","When it would be the only durable copy of business-critical data","When results can expire after an hour"],2,"Redis is an in-memory service best used as a fast cache or index; the durable source of truth should live in a database. Fast lookups and expiring data are good Redis fits.","Skills 2: Choosing a store"],["az46",0,2,"A developer builds an IVFFlat index and sees poor recall. Which change is most likely to help?",["Rebuild the index after loading the data and raise ivfflat.probes","Lower hnsw.ef_search","Switch the operator from <=> to @@","Decrease the lists value to 1 and set probes to 0"],0,"IVFFlat learns its lists from existing data, so building it on a full table and searching more lists (probes) improves recall. ef_search is an HNSW setting and @@ is full-text search.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector"],["az47",0,3,"An order service publishes each order once, and the billing, shipping and email services must each get their own copy. Which Service Bus setup fits?",["One queue with three competing receivers","A topic with three subscriptions","Three queues on the Basic tier with auto-forwarding","One queue with sessions enabled"],1,"A topic delivers a copy of each message to every subscription. Competing receivers on one queue split messages so each message goes to only one of them.","Skills 3: Service Bus"],["az48",0,3,"A receiver uses peek-lock. Processing fails on a message that is malformed and will never succeed. What should the code do?",["Call abandon_message so it can be retried forever","Call complete_message so it is removed silently","Call dead_letter_message with a reason","Let the lock expire"],2,"Dead-lettering a poison message moves it aside with a reason for inspection. Abandon or lock expiry only retry it, and complete loses it without a record.","Skills 3: Service Bus"],["az49",0,3,"Messages for the same customer must be processed in order, but different customers can be processed in parallel. Which feature should be used?",["Duplicate detection","Scheduled messages","Auto-forwarding","Sessions with SessionId set to the customer id"],3,"Sessions group messages by SessionId, and one receiver locks a session at a time, which keeps each customer's messages ordered while other sessions run in parallel.","Skills 3: Service Bus"],["az50",0,3,"A message keeps returning to the queue after failed attempts. After how many deliveries does Service Bus move it to the dead-letter queue by default?",["10","3","5","30"],0,"The default max delivery count is 10. After that the message is moved to the dead-letter subqueue.","Skills 3: Service Bus"],["az51",0,3,"A client may resend the same order message after a network timeout. How can Service Bus drop the duplicate automatically?",["Enable sessions on the queue","Enable duplicate detection and set a stable MessageId","Lower the lock duration","Use receive-and-delete mode"],1,"Duplicate detection discards messages with a MessageId already seen in the detection window. Sessions and receive modes do not remove duplicates.","Skills 3: Service Bus"],["az52",0,3,"A subscription should get only messages where the application property priority equals 'high'. What should be added?",["A second topic just for the high-priority messages from the sender","A session on the subscription","A subscription rule with a SQL filter priority = 'high'","Auto-forwarding from the topic"],2,"Subscription rules with SQL or correlation filters choose which messages a subscription receives. A second topic would require changing the sender.","Skills 3: Service Bus"],["az53",0,3,"A startup wants the cheapest Service Bus tier but needs publish-subscribe with topics. Which tier is the minimum?",["Basic","Premium","Standard","Any tier with partitioning enabled"],2,"Topics need at least Standard; Basic supports only queues. Premium works but is not the minimum.","Skills 3: Service Bus"],["az54",0,3,"A function must run when a blob is created in a storage account, and the team wants low-latency push delivery instead of polling. Which approach is recommended?",["A timer trigger that lists new blobs","A queue trigger reading storage logs","An HTTP trigger that the upload page calls from the browser","A blob trigger that uses an Event Grid source"],3,"The Event Grid-based blob trigger receives push notifications for new blobs with low latency. Timers and log reading are polling.","Skills 3: Azure Functions"],["az55",0,3,"An Event Grid subscription should deliver only BlobCreated events for files ending in .pdf. What should be configured?",["An event type filter plus a subject ends-with filter of .pdf","A dead-letter destination","A retry policy with 1 attempt","A CloudEvents schema on the topic"],0,"Event type and subject filters on the subscription limit what is delivered. Dead-lettering, retries and schema do not filter events.","Skills 3: Event Grid"],["az56",0,3,"A webhook subscriber was down for two days. Events from that time were lost. What should have been configured to keep them?",["A shorter event time-to-live","Dead-lettering to a Blob Storage container","A second custom topic","The Event Grid event schema instead of CloudEvents"],1,"When retries run out, Event Grid writes undelivered events to the dead-letter storage container if one is set. Without it they are dropped.","Skills 3: Event Grid"],["az57",0,3,"Which Event Grid behavior is correct for a failed delivery to a webhook?",["It retries once, then deletes the event","It retries with backoff until max attempts or the TTL expires","It stores the event in a Service Bus queue","It calls the webhook every second until the endpoint finally succeeds"],1,"Event Grid retries on a backoff schedule, bounded by max delivery attempts and event time-to-live (30 and 24 hours by default).","Skills 3: Event Grid"],["az58",0,3,"A telemetry pipeline ingests millions of device readings per second and must let several consumers replay the stream. Which service fits best?",["Event Grid custom topic","Service Bus queue","Azure Event Hubs","Azure Queue Storage"],2,"Event Hubs is a partitioned stream built for high-volume telemetry with replay by offset. Service Bus is for business messages, and Event Grid is for discrete events.","Skills 3: Choosing a service"],["az59",0,3,"A Python function should run every 15 minutes. Which schedule expression is correct for a timer trigger?",["*/15 * * * *","0 */15 * * * *","15 * * * * *","0 0 */15 * * *"],1,"Functions timer triggers use six-field NCRONTAB with seconds first: 0 */15 * * * * means second 0 of every 15th minute. The five-field form is missing seconds, and the last option runs every 15 hours.","Skills 3: Azure Functions"],["az60",0,3,"In the Python v2 programming model, where are triggers and bindings declared?",["In a function.json file next to each function","In host.json under extensions","In local.settings.json","As decorators on functions in function_app.py"],3,"The v2 model uses decorators on a FunctionApp object in function_app.py. function.json belongs to the v1 model, and host.json holds app-wide runtime settings.","Skills 3: Azure Functions"],["az61",0,3,"A Service Bus-triggered function must connect without a connection string. Which app setting shape enables an identity-based connection named ServiceBusConn?",["ServiceBusConn__fullyQualifiedNamespace = mynamespace.servicebus.windows.net","ServiceBusConn = Endpoint=sb://...;SharedAccessKey=...","AzureWebJobsServiceBus = managedidentity","ServiceBusConn__clientSecret = <secret>"],0,"The __fullyQualifiedNamespace suffix tells the extension to use an identity, and the identity needs a Service Bus data role. The second option is a key-based connection string.","Skills 3: Azure Functions"],["az62",0,3,"An HTTP-triggered function should require a key from callers but not an admin master key. Which auth level fits?",["anonymous","admin","function","system"],2,"The function level requires a function or host key. anonymous needs no key and admin needs the master key.","Skills 3: Azure Functions"],["az63",0,3,"A function app must scale to zero when idle but also reach private resources through a virtual network. Which hosting plan is designed for this?",["Flex Consumption","Dedicated on a Basic App Service plan","Premium with zero always-ready instances","Container Apps with min replicas 1"],0,"Flex Consumption supports scale to zero and virtual network integration. Premium keeps at least one instance warm, and Dedicated plans do not scale to zero.","Skills 3: Azure Functions hosting"],["az64",0,3,"A function should read an order from a Service Bus queue and write a document to Cosmos DB with no SDK code for the write. What should the developer use?",["A timer trigger and a Cosmos DB input binding","A Service Bus trigger and a Cosmos DB output binding","An HTTP trigger and a Service Bus output binding for the document","A Cosmos DB trigger and a queue input binding"],1,"The Service Bus trigger starts the function, and a Cosmos DB output binding writes the document. The other pairs have the wrong trigger or direction.","Skills 3: Azure Functions bindings"],["az65",0,3,"A developer runs a function app locally. Where should connection settings for local runs be stored?",["host.json","requirements.txt","local.settings.json (kept out of source control)","function_app.py as constants"],2,"local.settings.json holds app settings for local runs and should not be committed. In Azure, the same names are app settings on the function app.","Skills 3: Azure Functions"],["az66",0,3,"A Service Bus receiver takes several minutes per message and sometimes loses its lock, so the message is delivered twice. What should the developer do?",["Switch to the Basic tier","Enable duplicate detection on the queue with a ten-minute history window","Lower the max delivery count to 1","Renew the message lock during processing or use automatic lock renewal"],3,"Renewing the lock (the SDK's AutoLockRenewer in Python) keeps long processing from losing the message. Duplicate detection works on sends, not on redelivery.","Skills 3: Service Bus"],["az67",0,3,"A webhook endpoint is added as an Event Grid subscriber, but the subscription stays in a failed state. What is the most likely cause?",["The topic uses the CloudEvents schema instead of the Event Grid schema","The endpoint did not complete the subscription validation handshake","The subscription has no subject filter","Dead-lettering is disabled"],1,"Event Grid validates webhook endpoints before sending events; the endpoint must echo the validation code (or open the validation URL). Filters and dead-lettering are optional.","Skills 3: Event Grid"],["az68",0,4,"An app on Container Apps must read secrets from Key Vault. The same Python code must run on developer laptops with az login. Which credential class should it use?",["ClientSecretCredential with a stored secret","DefaultAzureCredential","UsernamePasswordCredential","AzureNamedKeyCredential"],1,"DefaultAzureCredential tries managed identity in Azure and developer sign-ins such as the Azure CLI locally, so the code does not change. The others need stored secrets or keys.","Skills 4: Managed identity"],["az69",0,4,"Five container apps must use one identity that is granted access to Key Vault before any of them is deployed. What should be used?",["A system-assigned identity on each app","A shared storage account key","A user-assigned managed identity","The Key Vault access policy for All Users"],2,"A user-assigned identity is created on its own, can be granted roles in advance and shared by several apps. System-assigned identities exist only once each app is created.","Skills 4: Managed identity"],["az70",0,4,"Using the Azure RBAC permission model, which role should an app's identity get to read secret values from Key Vault with least privilege?",["Key Vault Secrets User","Key Vault Administrator","Contributor","Key Vault Secrets Officer"],0,"Secrets User can read secret contents only. Secrets Officer can also create and delete, Administrator covers everything, and Contributor is management plane only.","Skills 4: Key Vault"],["az71",0,4,"A developer accidentally deletes a Key Vault secret that production needs. Which feature lets them get it back?",["Access policies","Private endpoint","Key rotation policy","Soft delete (recover the deleted secret)"],3,"Soft delete keeps deleted secrets for a retention period so they can be recovered. Purge protection also stops them being purged early.","Skills 4: Key Vault"],["az72",0,4,"An App Service app reads a database password from an app setting. The team wants the value to come from Key Vault without code changes. What should the setting contain?",["@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=https://myvault.vault.azure.net/secrets/DbPassword/)","keyvault://myvault/DbPassword","$(KeyVault.DbPassword)","The secret's version ID only"],0,"The @Microsoft.KeyVault(...) reference syntax makes App Service resolve the secret with the app's managed identity. The other forms are not recognized.","Skills 4: Key Vault references"],["az73",0,4,"A team wants to be alerted automatically before a Key Vault secret expires so a function can rotate it. What should they use?",["An Application Insights availability test that calls the vault each hour","The Key Vault SecretNearExpiry event through Event Grid","A Cosmos DB change feed","A timer trigger that calls the portal"],1,"Key Vault publishes SecretNearExpiry events to Event Grid, which can trigger a function to rotate the secret. Availability tests check web endpoints.","Skills 4: Key Vault rotation"],["az74",0,4,"Several apps need the same settings with different values per environment (dev, test, prod) and feature flags that change without redeploying. Which service fits?",["Key Vault with one vault per setting","App Service deployment slots","Azure App Configuration with labels and feature flags","Environment variables baked into each container image at build time"],2,"App Configuration stores key-values with labels per environment and supports feature flags and dynamic refresh. Baked-in variables require a rebuild to change.","Skills 4: App Configuration"],["az75",0,4,"An app loads App Configuration settings that include a Key Vault reference, but it fails to resolve the secret. The app can read App Configuration. What is missing?",["A label on the key","Soft delete on the vault","A sentinel key","Permission for the app's identity to read the secret in Key Vault"],3,"App Configuration stores only a pointer; the client fetches the secret from Key Vault with its own identity, so that identity needs a secret read role. Labels and sentinel keys do not affect access.","Skills 4: App Configuration"],["az76",0,4,"An app should reload many App Configuration settings only after a release is finished, not one key at a time. What pattern should be used?",["Poll every key every second","Watch a sentinel key and refresh all settings when it changes","Restart the app on every change","Store all settings as one JSON secret in Key Vault and read it at startup"],1,"Updating a single sentinel key after all changes signals the app to refresh everything at once, so it never sees a half-updated set.","Skills 4: App Configuration"],["az77",0,4,"A Python web API must send traces, metrics and logs to Application Insights using OpenTelemetry. What is the simplest supported setup?",["Install the Log Analytics agent on the container","Write to stdout and query ContainerLog","Use the classic instrumentation key with HTTP data collector","Call configure_azure_monitor() from azure-monitor-opentelemetry with the connection string"],3,"The Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry Distro sets up exporters and common instrumentations with one call and a connection string. Instrumentation-key-only ingestion is retired.","Skills 4: OpenTelemetry"],["az78",0,4,"A request passes through an API container, a Function and a database. Engineers want to see it as one end-to-end transaction in Application Insights. What makes this possible?",["Trace context propagated between services by OpenTelemetry","A separate Application Insights resource per service with no links","Sampling set to 0%","Writing the request ID into each log message by hand"],0,"OpenTelemetry passes W3C trace context in headers so spans share one trace ID and Application Insights can stitch them together. Sampling everything out would hide the data.","Skills 4: Distributed tracing"],["az79",0,4,"Which KQL query counts failed requests in 5-minute buckets over the last day?",["requests | where success == false | top 5 by timestamp","requests | where timestamp > ago(1d) and success == false | summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 5m)","exceptions | project timestamp | take 5","requests | summarize count() by success | where timestamp > ago(5m)"],1,"where filters the time and failures, and summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 5m) groups them into 5-minute buckets. top and take return rows, not counts per bucket.","Skills 4: KQL"],["az80",0,4,"Which Application Insights table records outgoing calls from the app to SQL, HTTP APIs and storage?",["requests","traces","dependencies","pageViews"],2,"dependencies holds outbound calls with their target, duration and result. requests holds incoming calls to the app, and traces holds log messages.","Skills 4: KQL"],["az81",0,4,"A team wants an email when the error count in logs goes above 50 in 10 minutes, based on a KQL query. What should they create?",["A log search alert rule with an action group","A metric alert on CPU percentage","An availability test","A diagnostic setting that streams the logs to Event Hubs"],0,"A log search alert runs a KQL query on a schedule and fires an action group when the threshold is met. A CPU metric alert does not count log errors.","Skills 4: Alerts"],["az82",0,4,"A Container App keeps restarting right after deployment. Where should the developer look first to see the app's own error output?",["The ACR task log","The Key Vault audit log","The Event Grid metrics blade","Container Apps console logs (log stream or ContainerAppConsoleLogs)"],3,"Console logs show what the container printed, including crash stack traces. System logs add platform events such as failed image pulls.","Skills 4: Troubleshooting"],["az83",0,4,"An app sends too much telemetry, and Application Insights costs are rising. Which change reduces volume while keeping traces representative?",["Turn off the connection string","Configure sampling","Log every request body","Create one Application Insights resource per instance"],1,"Sampling keeps a representative share of telemetry and correlated items together, cutting ingestion cost. Disabling telemetry loses monitoring entirely.","Skills 4: Monitoring"],["az84",0,4,"A function app's managed identity must send messages to a Service Bus queue. Which role is the least-privilege choice?",["Owner on the namespace","Azure Service Bus Data Owner","Azure Service Bus Data Sender","Contributor on the resource group"],2,"Data Sender allows sending only. Data Owner allows send, receive and manage, and Owner or Contributor are management roles.","Skills 4: RBAC"],["az85",0,4,"What is the key difference between Key Vault secrets and keys?",["Secrets can only be 32 bytes long","Keys are exported as plain text to the app","Secrets are always hardware-protected","Secret values are returned to the app, while key operations run inside the vault"],3,"Apps read secret values, but with keys they send data to the vault to encrypt, decrypt, sign or wrap, and the private key material stays in the vault.","Skills 4: Key Vault"],["az86",0,4,"Which statement about system-assigned managed identities is correct?",["They are deleted when the resource they belong to is deleted","They can be attached to many resources","They require a client secret in app settings","They must be rotated every 90 days by the developer using a script"],0,"A system-assigned identity shares the life cycle of its resource. User-assigned identities can be shared, and Azure handles credentials for both types.","Skills 4: Managed identity"],["az87",0,4,"A Python app deployed to App Service returns HTTP 500 errors, and the team needs to see live output while they reproduce the issue. What should they use?",["Deployment slot swap","Log stream after turning on application logging","ACR webhooks","The Cosmos DB metrics blade"],1,"The log stream shows application and web server logs in near real time once logging is enabled. Slot swaps change versions and do not show errors.","Skills 4: Troubleshooting"],["az88",0,4,"A developer wants to check that the Cosmos DB account allows data-plane access only with Microsoft Entra identities, not account keys. What should be configured?",["Session consistency on the account","A private endpoint only","Disable local (key-based) authentication and assign Cosmos DB data-plane roles","An App Configuration feature flag"],2,"Turning off local authentication forces Entra ID with data-plane role assignments. A private endpoint limits the network path but still allows keys.","Skills 4: RBAC"]]);
